# Read by every zsh, before every other startup file. Parameter assignments
# only: no output, no forks, nothing machine-specific -- every `zsh -c` in every
# script pays for whatever lands here.

# Ubuntu's /etc/zsh/zshrc runs a bare `compinit` before ~/.zshrc gets a say, and
# a bare compinit stops on "Ignore insecure directories [y/n]?" whenever an fpath
# entry is group-writable. It reads the answer from the terminal: on a tty the
# login blocks and swallows a keystroke, without one it aborts and the shell
# comes up with no completions. On this cluster the trigger is permanent -- lmod
# exports a group-writable directory owned by another uid into FPATH, and it is
# not ours to chmod. oh-my-zsh runs its own `compinit -i` later, so the global
# one is duplicated work everywhere else. Documented opt-out; see the comment
# above that block in /etc/zsh/zshrc.
#
# Deliberately not exported: the test there is on the plain parameter, and this
# file re-establishes it in every nested shell anyway.
skip_global_compinit=1
